<!--
  Device HAL manifest (VINTF) for the Meizu M5s on LineageOS 20.0.

  Why this file exists.  Without a DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE there is no
  /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml and hwservicemanager answers getTransport()
  with EMPTY for every vendor HAL.  Most clients survive that: system/libhidl's
  getRawServiceInternal() only warns on an undeclared interface and still tries
  hwbinder, then passthrough.

  Wi-Fi is the exception.  frameworks/opt/net/wifi HalDeviceManager decides
  whether a vendor HAL exists at all by asking the manifest directly:

      isSupportedInternal():
          IServiceManager.getTransport(IWifi.kInterfaceName, "default")
              != Transport.EMPTY

  With no manifest that returns EMPTY, WifiNative logs "Vendor Hal not
  supported, ignoring start", IWifi.start() is never called, and
  wifi_load_driver() never writes "1" into WIFI_DRIVER_STATE_CTRL_PARAM
  (/dev/wmtWifi).  The MTK combo driver therefore never powers up and wlan0 is
  never created.  Measured on the sibling m5c's live LOS 16 install 2026-09-03
  (M5C_LOS16_PERIPHERALS_MEASURE_20260903.md); the m5s runs the same CONSYS
  MT6735 combo and the same control path.

  Scope is deliberately narrow.  A HAL declared here but not actually served
  hangs its client (the m681 light@2.0 lesson).  The LOS 15.1 tree for this
  handset ships a 7.3 KB manifest listing a dozen HALs; that set is NOT copied
  here, because on this port none of those services is wired yet (see the
  "NOT WIRED YET" block in device.mk).  Add an entry only together with
  runtime proof that the service answers.

  OPEN, and stated rather than hidden: even these two entries are a HYPOTHESIS
  on the m5s.  On the m5c they were justified by lshal output from a running
  device; the m5s has never booted a forge image, so nothing here has been
  observed registered.  If the first boot hangs in WifiService, this file is
  the first thing to empty out.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.2</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IWifi</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <!--
      android.hardware.wifi.supplicant is deliberately NOT declared here
      (2026-09-24, lesson of m95 161682f).  The Android 13 wpa_supplicant of
      this tree serves only the AIDL ISupplicant (CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_AIDL,
      external/wpa_supplicant_8/wpa_supplicant/android.config:332) and declares
      it itself in its own VINTF fragment,
      /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.wifi.supplicant.xml
      (wpa_supplicant/Android.mk:1827).  The HIDL 1.1 entry that stood here
      would be a declared-but-unserved HAL (see the rule above).  The service
      itself is in rootdir/etc/init/init.m5s.wifi.rc.
    -->
</manifest>
